<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext xml:lang="en-US">
  <docinfo>
    <directories external="../assets"/>
    <macros>
      \newcommand{\C}{\mathbb C}
      \newcommand{\Pone}{\mathbb P^1_{\C}}
      \newcommand{\cO}{\mathcal O}
      \DeclareMathOperator{\ord}{ord}
    </macros>
  </docinfo>
  <book xml:id="divisors-p1">
    <title>Divisors and Line Bundles on the Projective Line</title>
    <shorttitle>Divisors on the projective line</shorttitle>
    <frontmatter xml:id="divisors-metadata">
      <bibinfo><author><personname>Jie Fu</personname></author></bibinfo>
      <titlepage><titlepage-items/></titlepage>
    </frontmatter>
    <chapter xml:id="divisors">
      <title>Divisors</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>Let <m>X=\Pone</m>. On its two affine charts, use coordinates <m>t=X_1/X_0</m> and <m>u=X_0/X_1</m>, with <m>u=1/t</m> on the overlap. This is the gluing description of <xref ref="stacks-projective-line"/>. Write <m>[a]</m> for the point <m>[1:a]</m> and <m>[\infty]</m> for <m>[0:1]</m>.<fn>In divisor expressions, brackets denote formal generators indexed by points. Homogeneous coordinates have two entries separated by a colon.</fn></p>
      </introduction>
      <section xml:id="divisor-degree">
        <title>Orders and degree</title>
        <subsection xml:id="local-orders">
          <title>Local orders</title>
          <p xml:id="anchor-divisor-orders">The local ring at a closed point <m>p</m> is a discrete valuation ring. For a nonzero rational function <m>g</m>, write <m>g=z_p^r v</m>, where <m>z_p</m> is a local parameter and <m>v</m> is a unit. The integer <m>r=\ord_p(g)</m> is independent of the parameter. Positive orders describe zeros and negative orders describe poles.</p>
          <aside xml:id="margin-divisor-orders">
            <p>The parameter at infinity is <m>u</m>, so <m>\ord_{\infty}(t)=-1</m>. A pole therefore contributes a negative coefficient to a principal divisor.</p>
          </aside>
          <p xml:id="anchor-divisor-degree">A divisor is a finite sum <m>D=\sum_p n_p[p]</m> with integer coefficients. Its degree is <m>\deg D=\sum_p n_p</m>. For a nonzero rational function, its principal divisor is <m>\operatorname{div}(g)=\sum_p\ord_p(g)[p]</m>; this sum has finite support.</p>
          <aside xml:id="margin-divisor-degree">
            <p>Divisor coefficients may be negative, so the degree can be any integer. For example, <m>\deg(-[\infty])=-1</m>.</p>
          </aside>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="principal-divisors">
          <title>Principal divisors</title>
          <p>The function <m>t-a</m> has one simple zero at <m>[a]</m>. At infinity it equals <m>(1-au)/u</m>, whose numerator is a unit. Hence</p>
          <md number="no">\operatorname{div}(t-a)=[a]-[\infty].</md>
          <p>Factorization over <m>\C</m> writes every nonzero rational function as <m>c\prod_a(t-a)^{m_a}</m>, with only finitely many nonzero integers <m>m_a</m>. Its divisor is <m>\sum_a m_a[a]-(\sum_a m_a)[\infty]</m>, so every principal divisor has degree zero.</p>
          <p>Divisors are linearly equivalent if their difference is principal. For <m>d=\deg D</m>, the formula above gives</p>
          <md number="no">D-d[\infty]=\operatorname{div}\left(\prod_a(t-a)^{n_a}\right).</md>
          <p>Thus every divisor is linearly equivalent to its degree times infinity. In <xref ref="prop-picard-p1"/>, this computation will classify all line bundles on <m>X</m>.</p>
        </subsection>
      </section>
    </chapter>
    <chapter xml:id="line-bundles">
      <title>Line bundles and sections</title>
      <section xml:id="divisor-bundles">
        <title>The bundle associated to a divisor</title>
        <p xml:id="anchor-divisor-bundle">For <m>D=\sum_p n_p[p]</m>, define <m>\cO_X(D)</m> to consist locally of rational functions <m>g</m> satisfying <m>\ord_p(g)+n_p\geq0</m>, together with zero. At <m>p</m>, its stalk is <m>z_p^{-n_p}\cO_{X,p}</m>, a free module of rank one. This gives a line bundle, using the divisor convention of <xref ref="hartshorne1977"/>.</p>
        <aside xml:id="margin-divisor-bundle">
          <p>If <m>n_p&gt;0</m>, the local frame <m>z_p^{-n_p}</m> has a pole as a rational function. It is a regular section of <m>\cO_X(D)</m>, since its order plus <m>n_p</m> is zero.</p>
        </aside>
        <p>Every line bundle <m>L</m> has a one-dimensional generic fibre over <m>\C(t)</m>. Choose a nonzero rational section <m>s</m> and write <m>s=h_pe_p</m> in local frames. The integers <m>\ord_p(h_p)</m> have finite support: <m>s</m> is regular and nonvanishing on a dense open set, whose complement is finite. Let <m>D=\operatorname{div}(s)</m>. Multiplication <m>g\mapsto gs</m> identifies <m>\cO_X(D)</m> with <m>L</m>, because locally <m>h_p=z_p^{n_p}v_p</m> with <m>v_p</m> a unit.</p>
        <p>Write <m>\cO_X(d)=\cO_X(d[\infty])</m>. The usual hyperplane bundle has a section <m>X_0</m> with divisor <m>[\infty]</m>. The preceding construction therefore identifies that bundle with <m>\cO_X(1)</m>.</p>
      </section>
      <section xml:id="picard-group">
        <title>The Picard group</title>
        <p>The Picard group consists of isomorphism classes of line bundles, with tensor product as its operation.</p>
        <p>The degree of a line bundle is the degree of the divisor of a nonzero rational section. The proof below checks that this definition is independent of the section and is compatible with tensor product.</p>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-picard-p1">
          <statement>
            <p>Degree gives an isomorphism <m>\operatorname{Pic}(X)\cong\mathbb Z</m>, whose inverse sends <m>d</m> to the class of <m>\cO_X(d)</m>.</p>
          </statement>
          <proof>
            <p>Two nonzero rational sections of the same bundle differ by a nonzero rational function. Their divisors differ by its principal divisor, so their degrees agree. This defines <m>\deg L</m>. For <m>L=\cO_X(D)</m>, the rational section one has divisor <m>D</m>: in the frame <m>z_p^{-n_p}</m> its coefficient is <m>z_p^{n_p}</m>.</p>
            <p>Every bundle is <m>\cO_X(D)</m> by the preceding construction. If <m>D-d[\infty]=\operatorname{div}(g)</m>, multiplication by <m>g</m> identifies <m>\cO_X(D)</m> with <m>\cO_X(d)</m>. Thus every class is represented by this bundle. Its degree is <m>d</m>, so distinct integers give distinct classes. Finally, multiplication of local frames gives <m>\cO_X(D)\otimes\cO_X(E)\cong\cO_X(D+E)</m>. Degree is therefore additive, proving the group isomorphism.</p>
          </proof>
        </proposition>
      </section>
      <section xml:id="divisor-global-sections">
        <title>Global sections</title>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-divisor-sections">
          <statement>
            <p>For every integer <m>d</m>, one has <m>\dim_{\C}H^0(X,\cO_X(d))=\max(d+1,0)</m>.</p>
          </statement>
          <proof>
            <p>A nonzero section is a rational function with no finite poles and with order at infinity at least <m>-d</m>. Write it as a reduced quotient of polynomials. A nonconstant denominator would have a complex root, producing a finite pole, so the function is a polynomial. A nonzero polynomial of degree <m>r</m> has order <m>-r</m> at infinity. Thus the allowed polynomials have degree at most <m>d</m>. For <m>d\geq0</m>, the monomials <m>1,t,\ldots,t^d</m> form a basis. For <m>d&lt;0</m>, only the zero section remains.</p>
          </proof>
        </proposition>
      </section>
    </chapter>
